<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Status-bar glyph for "an agent can act on this screen", and it ROTATES.

    Two decisions here, both forced by what the platform actually does rather than by taste:

    1. It is a different SHAPE, not a different colour. SystemUI draws every status-bar glyph
       through its own single foreground tint (for contrast against the wallpaper), so a
       Notification `setColor` reaches the shade badge and never the status bar. A colour that
       cannot render is not a state; a silhouette is. A broken ring around a solid core is
       unmistakably not the app's clay-flower mark at 18px.
    2. The rotation is an <animation-list>, i.e. the platform's own AnimationDrawable, and NOT an
       <animated-vector>. An AnimationDrawable with more than one frame starts itself out of
       `Drawable.setVisible(true, …)`, which is what ImageView (and therefore SystemUI's
       StatusBarIconView) calls when it takes the drawable — the same mechanism the framework's own
       `stat_sys_download` rides. An AnimatedVectorDrawable needs an explicit `start()` that nothing
       in the notification path issues, so it renders as a still frame. Neither costs a re-post: the
       animation is owned by the drawable, so nothing here wakes the app to keep it turning.

    12 frames x 200ms = one slow revolution every 2.4s.

    Frames are inlined through `aapt:attr` rather than 12 sibling files — the ONLY thing that varies
    is the ring group's rotation, and a dozen near-identical drawables invite one of them drifting.
-->
<animation-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:aapt="http://schemas.android.com/aapt"
    android:oneshot="false">

    <item android:duration="200">
        <aapt:attr name="android:drawable">
            <vector android:width="24dp" android:height="24dp"
                android:viewportWidth="24" android:viewportHeight="24">
                <path android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
                    android:pathData="M8.5,12a3.5,3.5 0 1 0 7,0a3.5,3.5 0 1 0 -7,0" />
                <group android:pivotX="12" android:pivotY="12" android:rotation="0">
                    <path android:strokeColor="#FFFFFFFF" android:strokeWidth="2.6"
                        android:strokeLineCap="round"
                        android:pathData="M12,3.5A8.5,8.5 0 1 1 3.5,12" />
                </group>
            </vector>
        </aapt:attr>
    </item>

